WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.405 --> 00:00:01.881
This is Charts.

2
00:00:01.831 --> 00:00:06.474
You can chart any coin or US stock, draw on it, add indicators, stack

3
00:00:06.474 --> 00:00:10.714
symbols to compare them, and save your charts to come back to.

4
00:00:11.375 --> 00:00:13.955
Cyrus opens the chart you had last.

5
00:00:13.905 --> 00:00:18.479
This demo starts on a saved chart: Bitcoin over Ethereum, on four

6
00:00:18.479 --> 00:00:23.054
hour candles, with a few lines already drawn and two indicators.

7
00:00:23.718 --> 00:00:25.261
Let's start a fresh one.

8
00:00:25.211 --> 00:00:28.123
Open the chart menu, and click New chart.

9
00:00:28.792 --> 00:00:31.691
A new chart starts on the symbol you were looking at.

10
00:00:31.641 --> 00:00:35.245
To change it, click the symbol's name at the top of the chart.

11
00:00:35.919 --> 00:00:38.273
First pick where the prices come from.

12
00:00:38.223 --> 00:00:42.545
Crypto works out of the box, from Coinbase, Kraken or KuCoin.

13
00:00:42.545 --> 00:00:46.946
Coinbase is the default, and it loads older candles as you scroll back.

14
00:00:47.612 --> 00:00:50.538
Stocks covers US stocks and ETFs.

15
00:00:50.488 --> 00:00:54.102
It uses your own free market data key from Alpaca, which

16
00:00:54.102 --> 00:00:57.136
you add once on your Profile, under Exchanges.

17
00:00:57.136 --> 00:01:02.336
Prices are real time, and volume counts trades on one exchange, called IEX.

18
00:01:03.022 --> 00:01:05.987
You can search by ticker or by company name.

19
00:01:05.937 --> 00:01:08.065
Type apple, and pick Apple.

20
00:01:08.742 --> 00:01:13.529
The buttons along the top set the timeframe, from one minute candles up to one week.

21
00:01:13.479 --> 00:01:16.405
Here's the daily chart, and then back to hourly.

22
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:23.380
The chart type menu draws the price as candles, bars, a line, or a filled area.

23
00:01:24.492 --> 00:01:31.340
The drawing tools run down the left side: lines and rays, channels, a fib retracement,

24
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:37.790
volume profiles, anchored VWAP, rectangles, a price range ruler, and text notes.

25
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:42.394
The buttons at the bottom hide, lock, or clear every drawing at once.

26
00:01:43.083 --> 00:01:46.740
Pick the trend line, then press and drag across the chart.

27
00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:53.512
Turn on the magnet first, and each end snaps to a nearby candle's open, high, low or close.

28
00:01:54.180 --> 00:02:01.028
A new drawing stays selected, and a style bar appears for it: its color, thickness, and dashes.

29
00:02:00.978 --> 00:02:04.263
You can also lock it, copy it, or delete it.

30
00:02:04.953 --> 00:02:06.216
Made a mistake?

31
00:02:06.166 --> 00:02:11.166
Undo and redo, at the top right, step back and forward through your changes.

32
00:02:11.166 --> 00:02:12.882
Control Z works too.

33
00:02:13.546 --> 00:02:15.355
Now a horizontal line.

34
00:02:15.305 --> 00:02:19.839
Turn the magnet off, pick the tool, and click once at the price you care about.

35
00:02:20.504 --> 00:02:23.762
Horizontal lines and trend lines can carry an alert.

36
00:02:23.712 --> 00:02:27.542
Click the bell, and Cyrus tells you when the price reaches the line.

37
00:02:27.542 --> 00:02:30.600
On a trend line, the alert follows the slope.

38
00:02:31.261 --> 00:02:34.067
Alerts work on stocks and crypto alike.

39
00:02:34.017 --> 00:02:39.141
Cyrus checks them about once a minute while it's running, and shows a desktop notification

40
00:02:39.141 --> 00:02:43.126
when one fires, plus an email if you've set email up on your Profile.

41
00:02:43.813 --> 00:02:45.887
The Alerts button lists them all.

42
00:02:45.837 --> 00:02:49.744
A fired alert switches off until you move its line or re-arm it,

43
00:02:49.744 --> 00:02:53.590
and moving or deleting the line takes the alert along with it.

44
00:02:54.261 --> 00:02:58.258
Indicators adds moving averages, Bollinger Bands,

45
00:02:58.258 --> 00:03:02.173
R S I, MACD, and a long list of volume studies.

46
00:03:02.123 --> 00:03:05.793
Each one is marked as drawing on the price, or in its own pane.

47
00:03:06.454 --> 00:03:08.302
Let's add Bollinger Bands.

48
00:03:08.252 --> 00:03:10.699
Its label appears at the top of the chart.

49
00:03:10.699 --> 00:03:15.646
Click the name or the gear to change its settings and color, or the cross to remove it.

50
00:03:16.317 --> 00:03:20.745
Compare adds another symbol to the same chart, up to four symbols in all.

51
00:03:20.695 --> 00:03:22.889
Let's put Microsoft under Apple.

52
00:03:23.985 --> 00:03:29.730
Each symbol gets its own pane, and they share one time axis, so you can line moves up.

53
00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.174
Any pane can come from a different source, and the cross in its header removes it.

54
00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:38.358
With two or more symbols, a new menu appears.

55
00:03:38.308 --> 00:03:41.598
Overlay in percent puts them on one chart, each measured

56
00:03:41.598 --> 00:03:44.770
as a percent change, so you can see which did better.

57
00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:48.082
Price overlays like the bands step aside in this mode.

58
00:03:48.754 --> 00:03:52.025
For your own indicators, open the Pine Editor.

59
00:03:51.975 --> 00:03:55.111
It's docked under the chart, and takes scripts written

60
00:03:55.111 --> 00:03:57.840
in Pine Script, the language TradingView uses.

61
00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.552
A new script starts from a simple example.

62
00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.590
Errors show up underneath as you type.

63
00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.304
Add to chart saves the script to your indicators, and draws it.

64
00:04:07.991 --> 00:04:11.134
If a script has alert conditions, its label gets a bell

65
00:04:11.134 --> 00:04:14.334
too, so you can be alerted when a condition comes true.

66
00:04:14.284 --> 00:04:16.372
Close the editor with the cross.

67
00:04:23.921 --> 00:04:27.538
The dot next to the chart's name means it has unsaved changes.

68
00:04:27.488 --> 00:04:32.900
Cyrus keeps them even if you switch to another chart, until you save or discard them.

69
00:04:33.588 --> 00:04:36.248
Click Save, or press Control S.

70
00:04:36.198 --> 00:04:40.865
A new chart asks for a name, and suggests one from its symbols and timeframe.

71
00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:46.460
A saved chart keeps its symbols, drawings, indicators and timeframe.

72
00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:53.325
The chart menu lists them all, and also starts a new chart, saves a copy, renames, or deletes.

73
00:04:53.325 --> 00:04:55.386
Let's go back to the Bitcoin chart.

74
00:04:56.067 --> 00:04:59.724
On a crypto chart, My trades shows your own activity.

75
00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:04.953
Dashed lines mark your open orders, with the side, the amount left, and the account.

76
00:05:04.953 --> 00:05:07.360
Arrows mark past buys and sells.

77
00:05:08.018 --> 00:05:11.874
If your average cost is in view, a solid line marks it too.

78
00:05:11.824 --> 00:05:14.218
The button turns it all off and on.

79
00:05:14.218 --> 00:05:18.673
Stock charts don't show any of this, because Cyrus doesn't trade stocks.

80
00:05:19.346 --> 00:05:24.918
The camera button saves a picture of the chart, with its name and symbols across the top.

81
00:05:25.563 --> 00:05:29.423
That's Charts: pick a coin or stock, mark it up, set alerts

82
00:05:29.423 --> 00:05:32.890
on the lines that matter, and save it for next time.

